Subject: [PATCH 2/5] =?UTF-8?q?fix(studio):=20CAM-01=20=E2=80=94=20the=20h?=
 =?UTF-8?q?alo=20around=20every=20object?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

MEASURED, NOT DESCRIBED

buildFloorMask decides floor-or-object for an 8×8 CELL, closes the result,
then dilates once more for margin. compositeFloor painted strictly by that
cell answer:

    if (mask.floor[cell] !== 1) continue;

So every boundary in the output was quantised to eight pixels and pushed a
further cell outward. Against objects whose edges are known by construction:

                        band of OLD floor left   worst   step per row   offsets
    vertical edge              16.0 px            16      0.00           1
    45° edge                   34.6 px            39      1.66          20
    shallow 18° edge           23.2 px            29      0.57          14

That band is the halo around every sofa, rug and chair leg in the output,
and the stepping is what reads as "blocky". On a 480px-wide frame, 35px is
seven percent of the picture.

WHAT CHANGES

The cell decision is right about the interior of an object and right about
open floor. It is only wrong within a cell or so of the boundary. So the
mask now marks that boundary — cells with both a floor and a non-floor cell
among their eight neighbours, grown two more rings to cover the full margin
the dilation created — and compositeFloor answers per pixel there and only
there, using THE SAME statistics the cell decision used rather than a second
opinion. Coverage comes back as 0..1 and is blended, because the edge of a
sofa against a floor is not a hard edge in the photograph either.

                        band of OLD floor left   worst   step per row
    vertical edge               1.0 px            1       0.00
    45° edge                    1.1 px            5       0.07
    shallow 18° edge            1.1 px            2       0.25

A sixteen- to thirty-five-pixel halo becomes one pixel, and the staircase
goes with it.

IT MAY NEVER BE MORE PERMISSIVE THAN THE RULE IT REFINES

The first version consulted chroma and RETURNED, skipping the luminance
test. The two objects in the fourteen-object fixture whose colour is closest
to wood immediately started losing their edges — the cream/jute rug to 6.3%
of its pixels painted, the brass lamp base to 14.9%. AUDIT-01 caught both.

Each ramp now reaches zero AT its threshold and full coverage a little
inside it, and both tests always apply. Every pixel the cell rule would have
called object is still refused; the ramp only softens pixels that were
already going to be painted. The margin this recovers is untouched by that,
because those cells are ordinary floor sitting well inside both tolerances —
they were excluded by the dilation, not by the test. AUDIT-01 passes
unchanged, 41 assertions, including every one of its named objects.

WHAT IT COSTS, STATED

The band is three rings around every object perimeter. On an empty floor it
is zero; with six objects in the quad it is 41% of the quad at 400×300 and
50% at 640×480 — NOT "a few percent", which is what the comment said first
and what the `border` band actually is. Frame cost at the live ladder's
rungs goes from roughly 12ms to 17ms at 400×300 and 31ms to 35ms at 640×480,
both inside the 41.7ms budget; where a device cannot hold it, LIVE_WIDTHS
already drops a rung after six slow frames, which is what that ladder is
for. It costs resolution on a slow phone, not frames.

Two rings was tried and rejected: 4.0px gap, 13px worst and the stepping
back at 1.47px per row on the 45° edge.

