From 8bf53249556614b4f2b9e18d14b4e147c750027c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 17:58:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] =?UTF-8?q?feat(api):=20RENDER-01=20=E2=80=94=20the=20?=
 =?UTF-8?q?room=20visualiser,=20as=20something=20to=20sell?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

WHAT IS ACTUALLY SELLABLE HERE

/api/v1 already exists — 27 endpoints and an OpenAPI document — and it is
open, unmetered and should stay that way: it exists to be crawled and cited,
and metering it would defeat the only reason it exists. It is not a product
you can charge for and was never meant to be.

The renderer is. compositeFloor and analyseRoom take a photograph of a room
and a floor specification and return the room with that floor in it. Other
flooring retailers pay for exactly this. And the code is already pure — no
React, no Next, no Prisma anywhere in the path — which is what makes it
sellable to somebody else's website without lifting it out of this
repository.

This service IMPORTS those functions. It does not copy them. A second copy
is how the product a customer pays for and the product on ecowoods.ca stop
being the same thing.

WHY FLY AND NOT A VERCEL FUNCTION — MEASURED

    image        decode   render   encode    total   renders/min/core
    640x480        11ms     62ms     23ms     96ms        624
    1280x960       35ms    190ms     84ms    309ms        194
    1600x1200      66ms    301ms    129ms    496ms        121
    2048x1536      85ms    561ms    215ms    862ms         69

That is CPU, not waiting. A serverless function bills wall-clock, cold-starts
per request and caps out; a Fly machine is a warm process that holds the
catalogue in memory and costs the same whether it renders once or ten
thousand times. primary_region is yyz — Toronto, where the customers are.
shared-cpu-2x/2GB was $10.70 a month when this was written.

ZERO DEPENDENCIES, AND THAT IS NOT A FLOURISH

This repository installs with `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile`. One new
package anywhere in the workspace makes pnpm-lock.yaml stale and fails that
command for everyone — which is why SALE-04 sent Twilio through fetch rather
than its SDK. So the HTTP server is node:http, auth is node:crypto, and the
PNG codec is node:zlib. There is no build step to go wrong between here and
production.

src/png.ts is the only real work in that: encode and decode, ~160 lines,
proven PIXEL-EXACT against sharp in BOTH directions and across all five PNG
scanline filters, RGB and RGBA. 16-bit, palette and interlaced are refused
BY NAME rather than returning something subtly wrong. JPEG cannot be decoded
without a dependency and is not pretended to be — the README gives the one
line of canvas that converts it.

TWO THINGS THE RUNTIME CAUGHT THAT A REVIEW WOULD NOT

  1. `constructor(private readonly now = Date.now)` — a TypeScript parameter
     property. Node's strip-only type stripping cannot emit the assignment it
     implies and refuses the file outright. It runs that way in the container,
     with no build step to paper over it.
  2. The loader must return `module-typescript`, not `module`. `module` tells
     Node the file is already JavaScript, so types are never stripped and it
     fails on the first `import type {` — reported as a syntax error deep
     inside room.ts, which is a confusing place to look for a loader bug.
     Relative imports need resolving too: `./catalog` is not a file.

WHAT MAKES IT A PRODUCT RATHER THAN A GIVEAWAY

An API you sell needs a caller identity, a limit, and a record you can bill
from. Keys live in RENDER_API_KEYS as id:sha256:per-minute; the secret is
never stored, only its hash, so a leaked environment does not leak a working
key, and the comparison is timing-safe because a plain === on a secret leaks
its prefix to anyone willing to measure an endpoint that answers in
milliseconds. Every request writes one JSON line with key, route, status,
duration and pixels.

VERIFIED

  22 unit assertions on the auth and the meter, mutation-tested: removing the
  timing-safe compare fails 2, removing the limit check fails 1.

  A live end-to-end run against the real server: open routes without a key,
  401 on no key / wrong secret / unknown id, /v1/analyse returning a room
  reading and a quad, /v1/render returning a PNG that is measurably repainted,
  400-by-name on an unknown species and on a corrupt image, 429 over the
  limit, and a usage line per request.

STATED RATHER THAN IMPLIED, IN THE README

Billing reads the log stream — that is a real record and it is not a ledger;
the durable table is the next slice and the line is already the shape of a
row. The rate limiter is per process, which is why fly.toml pins
max_machines_count = 1. Nothing is stored: the photograph is decoded,
rendered and dropped, which follows the PIPEDA reasoning already in room.ts
and is worth telling a customer because it is their liability too.

And /v1/analyse does NOT return a square footage. A single uncalibrated
photograph with no object of known size in it cannot yield an area. The
customer is asked.

65 of 66 guards pass; verify:css cannot run in this sandbox (postcss
unresolvable, npm 403) and fails identically on a clean checkout. parse-scan
clean, repo hygiene clean.

